  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe Nor'easter continues to spin off the coast of New Jersey,\nwith the low center beginning to move slowly westward through\nthe overnight hours. As it moves right along the coastline this\nmorning, it will push a heavier rain band north and west, moving\nacross NE PA and Central NY slowly through the day. Areas in NE\nPA and the Catskills are expected to see heavier rainfall first,\nwith the rain band pushing north and west through the day.\nRainfall amounts have been the most difficult forecase-wise,\nwhich was largely dependent on the speed of the main rain band\nsupplying the vast majority of the amount. The biggest change\nhas been decreasing rainfall amounts a little bit, which ranges\nfrom a half to three-quarters inch for NE PA and the Catskills,\nand a quarter to a half inch for areas in Central NY, which is\nexpected to fall over the course of 24 hours. Chances for \nflooding and hydrological issues still remains very low.\n\nAs the center low pushes up along the NJ coast, the pressure\ngradient will tighten, and gusty winds remain expected through\nthe day Sunday. Wind speeds are still expected to remain below\nAdvisory criteria.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure will build into the region by Tuesday and bring a \nstretch of quieter conditions. With some weak ridging, peaks of\nsunshine, and southwest to westerly flow will help bump up the \ntemperatures midweek with daytime highs near or in the 70s for\nthe majority of the mid-week.\n\nThese pleasant conditions will only last a few days as rain looks to \nreturn late in the week. While models differ on the timing and set \nup, a boundary will extend from the Midwest into the Northeast with \nshowers along it. Where this boundary sets up will determine the \ncoverage of showers and if conditions dry out in time to start next \nweekend. Given the uncertainty, this forecast will maintain low \nchance PoPs late in the week.",
